{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>It shall stand alive.</b> One needs to know the intention, and our Sages of blessed memory (Yoma 67a; Torat Kohanim) derived many homiletical interpretations. According to our approach it shall be explained correctly that in saying ״יָעֳמַד חַי״ the intention is that he should not confess over it immediately and at once when the lot falls upon it, but rather he should leave it alive — meaning, for the reason that the aspect of evil is called dead, and on account of it a body without a soul is called dead, because the aspect of impurity called dead holds sway over it. And the goat of Azazel, when the Kohen confesses over it and places upon it all the iniquities and transgressions etc. — behold, it is dead, encompassing all manner of deaths. And the Master commanded that he not make it dead, but rather ״יָעֳמַד חַי״ — it shall stand alive. And therefore you will find that Scripture says afterward <b>וסמך אהרן וגו׳ על ראש השעיר החי והתודה</b> etc. <b>ונשא השעיר עליו</b> — for at first, before he confessed, it called it חי (alive), and afterward it said ״השעיר״ (the goat) and did not mention ״החי״ (the alive one), to say that after he confessed over it, it is no longer alive, for the forces of death have already descended upon it. And you will find that HaShem commanded that the one who sends it is impure and shall wash his garments and bathe his flesh in the water — according to the law of one impure through the dead."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:33.812700914Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}